---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../config';
---

<DocsLayout
	title="Documentation"
	description={`How ${site.name} is built, edited and extended.`}
>
	<p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
	<h1>Getting started</h1>
	<p class="lede">
		A static Astro front end on headless WordPress, with a signed-in dashboard for subscriptions and email
		marketing.
	</p>

	<h2>How the pieces fit</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Piece</th><th>Job</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>WordPress + MySQL</td><td>Stores content and user accounts. Editors work here.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>WPGraphQL</td><td>Read API the site builds from.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Meta Box</td><td>Custom fields on content.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Astro</td><td>Renders public pages as static HTML, plus the on-demand dashboard.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Stripe / PayPal</td><td>Subscriptions. No WooCommerce anywhere.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Emailit</td><td>Transactional email and campaigns.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		WordPress is never public-facing: visiting it redirects here, and its admin "View" links point at this site.
	</p>

	<h2>Signing in</h2>
	<p>
		Go to <a href="/login">/login</a> and use the same credentials as WordPress — there is no separate sign-up
		table. Roles come straight from WordPress.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Role</th><th>Sees</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Everything, including Marketing, Users and Health</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Subscriber / other</td><td>Overview, Billing, Account. Admin pages return 404.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<div class="callout">
		<p>
			Sessions store only your user id. Roles and subscription are re-read from WordPress on every request, so
			changing a role in wp-admin takes effect on the next page load rather than whenever a session expires.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Editing content</h2>
	<p>Everything public is built from WordPress:</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>In WordPress</th><th>On the site</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>Pages</td><td><code>/about</code> — nested pages keep their path</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Posts</td><td><code>/blog/&lt;slug&gt;</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>Work → Projects</td><td><code>/work/&lt;slug&gt;</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>Services</td><td><code>/services/&lt;slug&gt;</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td>Testimonials</td><td>Rendered inside other pages</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		The boxes below the editor — Project Details, Hero, SEO &amp; Social — are Meta Box fields, available over both
		GraphQL and REST. See the <a href="/docs/api">API reference</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		In development the dev server picks up edits within about five seconds. In production, publishing fires a build
		hook so the static site rebuilds.
	</p>

	<h2>Adding a field, end to end</h2>
	<p>A custom field touches five places. Miss one and it fails loudly rather than silently:</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>#</th><th>File</th><th>Why</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>1</td><td><code>wordpress/mu-plugins/app-fields.php</code></td><td>Defines the field</td></tr>
			<tr><td>2</td><td><code>web/src/lib/wp/queries.ts</code></td><td>Asks for it</td></tr>
			<tr><td>3</td><td><code>web/src/lib/wp/schema.ts</code></td><td>Validates and normalises it</td></tr>
			<tr><td>4</td><td><code>web/scripts/mock-wp.mjs</code></td><td>Keeps the offline mock honest</td></tr>
			<tr><td>5</td><td><code>wordpress/tests/schema-contract.php</code></td><td>Asserts the names still line up</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>Then check nothing drifted:</p>
	<pre><code>php wordpress/tests/schema-contract.php
cd web &amp;&amp; npm run mock:validate &amp;&amp; npm run check</code></pre>

	<h2>Working without WordPress</h2>
	<p>A mock GraphQL server serves the same schema from fixtures, so the front end runs with nothing else installed:</p>
	<pre><code>cd web
npm run mock        # terminal 1
npm run dev:mock    # terminal 2</code></pre>

	<h2>Coming soon &amp; maintenance</h2>
	<p>
		Set the mode in WordPress under <strong>Settings → App Settings</strong>: Live, Coming soon (HTTP 200, so it can
		be indexed) or Maintenance (HTTP 503 with <code>Retry-After</code>, so search engines keep the existing listing).
	</p>
	<div class="callout">
		<p>
			<strong>Public pages are gated at build time, not instantly.</strong> Prerendered files are served straight
			off disk and never reach the server's middleware, so the gate is applied when the site is <em>built</em>.
			Saving the setting fires the build hook to trigger that rebuild. Dynamic routes — <code>/login</code>,
			<code>/dashboard</code>, <code>/api</code> — gate immediately and stay reachable in every mode, so you can
			always sign in and turn it off.
		</p>
	</div>
	<p>
		Setting <code>SITE_MODE</code> in <code>web/.env</code> overrides WordPress in either direction: use
		<code>live</code> to keep working locally while production is gated, or <code>maintenance</code> to gate the site
		when WordPress itself is down.
	</p>

	<h2>Health check</h2>
	<p>
		Administrators get a <a href="/dashboard/health">Health</a> tab that verifies the whole chain live: database
		tables and row counts, each content type's mapping through GraphQL and REST to an Astro collection and route,
		whether API keys and webhooks are registered, and whether real data is coming back. Start there when something
		looks wrong.
	</p>
</DocsLayout>
